# What must not enter the build context.
#
# The build context is everything Docker can see while building. Two reasons to
# keep it small: nothing outside it can be copied in by accident, and the whole
# context is sent to the Docker daemon before the build starts, so a 30 MB
# corpus is 30 MB of waiting on every build.
#
# The Dockerfile copies named files rather than `COPY . .`, so most of this is a
# second line of defence. The first two entries are the ones that matter.

# Secrets. Never in an image: a file copied into a layer stays in that layer
# even if a later instruction deletes it, so the key would travel with every
# copy of the image. Keys are a run-time input, supplied by Compose.
.env
.env.*
!.env.example

# A virtualenv built for macOS, holding macOS binaries. Inside a Linux image it
# is both broken and large. The image builds its own with `uv sync`.
.venv

# The corpus: third-party documents, deliberately untracked, and only ever read
# by `ingest` — which does not run in this image.
data/

# History and tooling state. Nothing here is needed to run the API.
.git
.github
.gitignore
.ruff_cache
.pytest_cache
.mypy_cache
.coverage
htmlcov/
**/__pycache__
**/*.pyc

# Editor and OS noise.
.DS_Store
.idea
.vscode

# Written work. Read by people, not by the server.
docs/
**/designs/
**/investigations/
**/README.md
!README.md

# Tests and their fixtures. pytest is not installed in the image (--no-dev), and
# the image is not how the suite is run.
**/tests/
**/conftest.py

# Docker's own files.
Dockerfile
**/Dockerfile
.dockerignore
docker-compose.yml
